1
00:00:00,106 --> 00:00:02,606
You show a photo to an image classifier.

2
00:00:03,031 --> 00:00:04,454
It does not just answer cat.

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:12,061
It answers: cat with zero point nine, dog with zero point zero eight, fox with zero point zero two.

4
00:00:12,486 --> 00:00:17,606
A language model does the same for the next word, over tens of thousands of words.

5
00:00:18,031 --> 00:00:23,390
These lists of chances are probability distributions, and they follow a few strict rules.

6
00:00:24,313 --> 00:00:35,297
In this video you will build a distribution by counting, see how continuous chances become areas, and learn the two rules of probability: the sum rule and the product rule.

7
00:00:35,722 --> 00:00:39,791
At the end, you will derive Bayes' theorem in three lines.

8
00:00:40,230 --> 00:00:42,996
You need video eleven, random variables.

9
00:00:43,435 --> 00:00:46,693
This is axiom D, the probability rules.

10
00:00:48,393 --> 00:00:52,728
Let's go back to two fair dice and the random variable X, their sum.

11
00:00:53,167 --> 00:00:56,039
There are thirty six equally likely outcomes.

12
00:00:56,478 --> 00:00:58,725
Count how many outcomes give each sum.

13
00:00:59,124 --> 00:01:00,799
A sum of two has one outcome.

14
00:01:01,225 --> 00:01:03,047
A sum of three has two.

15
00:01:03,486 --> 00:01:08,778
The counts grow up to seven, which has six, and then they shrink back to one for twelve.

16
00:01:09,706 --> 00:01:15,224
Divide every count by thirty six, and you get a probability for every value.

17
00:01:15,637 --> 00:01:22,100
The probability that X equals two is one over thirty six, about zero point zero two eight.

18
00:01:22,538 --> 00:01:29,413
The probability that X equals seven is six over thirty six, about zero point one six seven.

19
00:01:29,839 --> 00:01:35,863
This complete list of values and their probabilities is called the probability distribution of X.

20
00:01:37,359 --> 00:01:39,939
Every distribution follows two rules.

21
00:01:40,418 --> 00:01:43,250
First, no probability is negative.

22
00:01:43,689 --> 00:01:46,787
Second, all the probabilities add up to one.

23
00:01:47,226 --> 00:01:54,593
Check it here: one plus two plus three, and so on up to six and back down to one, gives thirty six.

24
00:01:55,099 --> 00:01:57,572
Thirty six over thirty six is one.

25
00:01:58,011 --> 00:02:01,029
Something must happen, so the total chance is one.

26
00:02:01,959 --> 00:02:04,858
Compare this with the simulation from video eleven.

27
00:02:05,297 --> 00:02:14,207
There, the sum seven appeared one thousand six hundred eighty times in ten thousand rolls: a share of zero point one six eight.

28
00:02:14,659 --> 00:02:18,555
The distribution says zero point one six seven.

29
00:02:19,007 --> 00:02:21,348
The histogram of samples is an estimate.

30
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:24,699
The distribution is the exact rule behind it.

31
00:02:26,413 --> 00:02:31,958
Now a continuous random variable: the waiting time for a bus that comes every ten minutes.

32
00:02:32,397 --> 00:02:35,987
Every time between zero and ten is equally likely.

33
00:02:36,413 --> 00:02:40,788
We cannot list the values, so instead we draw a curve called a density.

34
00:02:41,226 --> 00:02:45,641
Here it is a flat line at height zero point one over the ten minutes.

35
00:02:46,639 --> 00:02:50,615
For a density, probability is area under the curve.

36
00:02:51,054 --> 00:02:54,471
What is the chance of waiting between two and five minutes?

37
00:02:54,910 --> 00:03:01,440
The area is a rectangle: width three times height zero point one, which is zero point three.

38
00:03:01,852 --> 00:03:06,652
And the area under the whole curve is ten times zero point one, which is one.

39
00:03:07,078 --> 00:03:08,275
The total is one again.

40
00:03:09,793 --> 00:03:13,968
Many real quantities, like human height, have a bell-shaped density.

41
00:03:14,394 --> 00:03:19,979
The idea is the same: the probability of a range is the area of that slice.

42
00:03:20,418 --> 00:03:28,117
A single exact value, like exactly one hundred seventy point zero zero zero centimeters, has no width.

43
00:03:28,543 --> 00:03:30,710
So its probability is zero.

44
00:03:31,109 --> 00:03:32,665
We always ask about ranges.

45
00:03:34,373 --> 00:03:38,282
Machine learning almost always has more than one random variable.

46
00:03:38,721 --> 00:03:39,971
So let's look at two at once.

47
00:03:40,396 --> 00:03:41,553
This is a toy example.

48
00:03:41,992 --> 00:03:47,963
Each morning we note the weather, rain or no rain, and whether a person carries an umbrella.

49
00:03:48,362 --> 00:03:52,152
A joint table gives the probability of every pair of values.

50
00:03:53,073 --> 00:03:53,817
Read the table.

51
00:03:54,269 --> 00:03:58,724
The probability of rain and umbrella together is zero point two five.

52
00:03:59,123 --> 00:04:02,860
Rain and no umbrella is zero point zero five.

53
00:04:03,272 --> 00:04:06,184
No rain and umbrella is zero point one.

54
00:04:06,596 --> 00:04:09,961
No rain and no umbrella is zero point six.

55
00:04:10,386 --> 00:04:14,442
The four cells cover every possible morning, so they add up to one.

56
00:04:16,239 --> 00:04:16,877
First rule.

57
00:04:17,303 --> 00:04:21,266
What is the probability of rain, no matter what happens with the umbrella?

58
00:04:21,678 --> 00:04:28,008
Add across the rain row: zero point two five plus zero point zero five gives zero point three.

59
00:04:28,433 --> 00:04:32,901
This is the sum rule: to forget a variable, add over all its values.

60
00:04:33,327 --> 00:04:35,002
The result is called a marginal.

61
00:04:35,939 --> 00:04:43,266
The other row gives the probability of no rain: zero point one plus zero point six is zero point seven.

62
00:04:43,692 --> 00:04:51,179
Adding down the columns gives the umbrella: zero point three five, and no umbrella: zero point six five.

63
00:04:51,604 --> 00:04:58,466
Each marginal is again a distribution, because zero point three plus zero point seven is one.

64
00:04:59,773 --> 00:05:00,703
Now a new question.

65
00:05:01,102 --> 00:05:04,241
If we know that it rains, how likely is the umbrella?

66
00:05:04,666 --> 00:05:08,323
We keep only the rain row and rescale it so it adds up to one.

67
00:05:08,749 --> 00:05:14,254
Zero point two five divided by zero point three is about zero point eight three three.

68
00:05:14,679 --> 00:05:19,081
This is a conditional probability, written p of umbrella given rain.

69
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:20,544
The bar means given.

70
00:05:21,473 --> 00:05:24,651
Turn that division around, and you get the second rule.

71
00:05:25,090 --> 00:05:34,491
The joint probability equals the conditional probability times the marginal: p of x and y equals p of y given x, times p of x.

72
00:05:34,744 --> 00:05:40,502
Check it: zero point eight three three times zero point three is zero point two five.

73
00:05:40,927 --> 00:05:42,151
This is the product rule.

74
00:05:43,873 --> 00:05:47,396
Sometimes one variable tells us nothing about the other.

75
00:05:47,835 --> 00:05:56,293
Then p of y given x is just p of y, and the product rule becomes p of x and y equals p of x times p of y.

76
00:05:56,718 --> 00:05:58,367
This is called independence.

77
00:05:58,806 --> 00:06:00,455
Flip a coin and roll a die.

78
00:06:00,881 --> 00:06:06,107
The chance of heads and a six is one half times one sixth, one twelfth.

79
00:06:08,239 --> 00:06:10,420
Are rain and umbrella independent?

80
00:06:10,846 --> 00:06:18,186
Multiply the marginals: zero point three times zero point three five is zero point one zero five.

81
00:06:18,612 --> 00:06:21,324
But the table says zero point two five.

82
00:06:21,763 --> 00:06:25,287
They are not equal, so the variables are not independent.

83
00:06:25,726 --> 00:06:29,808
Of course: seeing an umbrella tells you something about the weather.

84
00:06:31,339 --> 00:06:32,150
Now the payoff.

85
00:06:32,589 --> 00:06:35,129
The product rule works in both directions.

86
00:06:35,568 --> 00:06:40,209
p of x and y equals p of y given x times p of x.

87
00:06:40,634 --> 00:06:44,291
It also equals p of x given y times p of y.

88
00:06:44,717 --> 00:06:45,980
Both right sides are equal.

89
00:06:46,406 --> 00:06:54,065
Divide by p of y, and you get p of x given y equals p of y given x times p of x, over p of y.

90
00:06:55,006 --> 00:06:58,955
That is Bayes' theorem, derived in three lines from the product rule.

91
00:06:59,394 --> 00:07:00,884
It turns a conditional around.

92
00:07:01,309 --> 00:07:03,463
We know p of umbrella given rain.

93
00:07:03,902 --> 00:07:14,673
Bayes gives p of rain given umbrella: zero point eight three three times zero point three, over zero point three five, about zero point seven one four.

94
00:07:15,099 --> 00:07:17,798
Bayes is a molecule, built from this atom.

95
00:07:19,493 --> 00:07:22,564
In machine learning, these ideas are everywhere.

96
00:07:22,963 --> 00:07:26,447
A classifier outputs a distribution over classes.

97
00:07:26,886 --> 00:07:32,564
The probabilities are never negative and they add up to one, exactly like the dice table.

98
00:07:32,990 --> 00:07:38,961
Often it is a conditional distribution: the probability of each class, given the input photo.

99
00:07:40,506 --> 00:07:45,838
A language model outputs a distribution over the next token, given all the words so far.

100
00:07:46,251 --> 00:07:54,854
To write a whole text, it chains these conditionals with the product rule: the probability of a sentence is a product of next word probabilities.

101
00:07:55,293 --> 00:07:58,086
That is why the logs from video ten are so useful.

102
00:07:59,606 --> 00:08:03,077
And independence is the usual assumption about training data.

103
00:08:03,502 --> 00:08:07,651
We assume the examples are independent and come from the same distribution.

104
00:08:08,103 --> 00:08:13,090
Then the probability of the whole data set is a product of the probabilities of the examples.

105
00:08:13,529 --> 00:08:17,744
Without this assumption, most loss functions would be much harder to write.

106
00:08:19,472 --> 00:08:20,004
Your turn.

107
00:08:20,430 --> 00:08:22,531
Use the rain and umbrella table.

108
00:08:22,970 --> 00:08:26,294
What is the probability of no umbrella, given rain?

109
00:08:26,733 --> 00:08:28,156
Pause the video and try.

110
00:08:28,595 --> 00:08:30,523
Hint: keep only the rain row.

111
00:08:31,439 --> 00:08:37,902
The rain row is zero point two five and zero point zero five, and it adds up to zero point three.

112
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:41,825
Divide zero point zero five by zero point three.

113
00:08:42,263 --> 00:08:44,923
You get about zero point one six seven.

114
00:08:45,348 --> 00:08:54,484
Check: zero point eight three three plus zero point one six seven is one, as a conditional distribution must be.

115
00:08:56,006 --> 00:08:56,857
Second question.

116
00:08:57,295 --> 00:08:58,306
Two fair dice.

117
00:08:58,745 --> 00:09:01,843
What is the probability that the sum is at least eleven?

118
00:09:02,282 --> 00:09:03,878
Use the distribution table.

119
00:09:04,303 --> 00:09:05,008
Pause and try.

120
00:09:05,939 --> 00:09:09,317
A sum of eleven has probability two over thirty six.

121
00:09:09,755 --> 00:09:12,561
A sum of twelve has one over thirty six.

122
00:09:12,987 --> 00:09:22,109
These events cannot happen together, so we add them: three over thirty six, which is one twelfth, about zero point zero eight three.

123
00:09:23,839 --> 00:09:25,621
Let's check everything in NumPy.

124
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:31,073
We build all thirty six dice outcomes with add dot outer, and count each sum.

125
00:09:31,498 --> 00:09:33,759
Then we write the joint table as an array.

126
00:09:34,171 --> 00:09:37,217
A sum along each row gives the marginals.

127
00:09:37,642 --> 00:09:41,592
Dividing the rain row by its total gives the conditional.

128
00:09:42,030 --> 00:09:46,020
And the outer product of the marginals shows what independence would look like.

129
00:09:47,539 --> 00:09:51,395
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

130
00:09:51,821 --> 00:09:57,459
The dice counts are one to six and back to one, and the probabilities add up to one.

131
00:09:57,898 --> 00:10:05,478
The marginals are zero point three and zero point seven, and zero point three five and zero point six five.

132
00:10:05,903 --> 00:10:08,643
The product rule gives back zero point two five.

133
00:10:09,055 --> 00:10:12,419
Independence would give zero point one zero five.

134
00:10:12,845 --> 00:10:15,478
And Bayes gives zero point seven one four.

135
00:10:17,172 --> 00:10:17,797
In summary.

136
00:10:18,223 --> 00:10:23,741
A probability distribution lists every value of a random variable with its probability.

137
00:10:24,167 --> 00:10:27,651
The probabilities are never negative and add up to one.

138
00:10:28,103 --> 00:10:33,130
For a continuous variable, a density gives probability as area.

139
00:10:33,555 --> 00:10:37,079
With two variables, the joint table holds everything.

140
00:10:38,026 --> 00:10:49,063
The sum rule gives marginals, the product rule links joint and conditional probabilities, and independence means p of x and y equals p of x times p of y.

141
00:10:49,808 --> 00:10:52,042
Bayes' theorem follows in three lines.

142
00:10:52,467 --> 00:10:59,183
Next, in video thirteen: the expected value, the long-run average of a random variable.

143
00:11:01,106 --> 00:11:02,927
This video was made by David R.

144
00:11:03,366 --> 00:11:06,598
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

145
00:11:07,156 --> 00:11:07,874
Thanks for watching.
